+## Current learnability observation: square examples leave tile aspect ambiguous
+
+Source SHA-256: `d9f0a1782d0c3e9d5365e03779f8b58e876a1f2af751adf3f8dc62f9c68371b4`.
+Canonical program SHA-256: `6ccc058b9b2eefbf6cbf155e33ddde07633fa68196b7b614a4ad407b24acd52b`.
+
+All three visible canonical demonstrations are square (9×9, 6×6, 6×6).
+A simple rival divides the input into square tiles of side `height/3` and
+emits one cell per tile, preserving the number of horizontal tiles. It agrees
+with all three demonstrations. Eight live protected-Racket evaluations
+verified both programs on those demonstrations and one generated query.
+
+The lawful query is `generate(seed=731, sample_index=0, difficulty="medium",
+block_h=2, block_w=4)`: a 6×12 grid with exactly one nonzero in each of its nine
+2×4 blocks, all nine foreground colors, and `self_check == []`. The canonical
+Racket output is `[[4,1,3],[2,6,9],[7,8,5]]`; the square-tile rival instead
+outputs `[[4,0,1,0,3,0],[2,0,6,0,0,9],[7,0,8,0,5,0]]`.
+
+This is a bounded ambiguity in the visible square demonstrations, not a
+canonical-label or Racket defect. Including a rectangular demonstration when
+rectangular queries are used would distinguish this rival. No domain expansion
+is required, and no production generator or rule changed.
+
+Reproduce from packet root with
+`WORK/.venv/bin/python review.py run python3 ../checks/d1c037cda16e/rectangular_probe.py`
+(exit 0). Source-bound inputs, complete Racket programs, outputs and runtime
+identity are in `checks/d1c037cda16e/rectangular_probe.json`; the reproducible
+probe is `checks/d1c037cda16e/rectangular_probe.py` (both relative to WORK).
+Only one generated query was tested. No full battery, seed sweep, parametric
+qualification, old-audit replay or training admission was performed. The
+existing override/validator audit remains unchanged and unresolved.
